        "orario": "19:19",
        "timestamp": 1779297582,
        "contenuto_esteso": "Advertisement United States World United States & Canada Harvard to cap number of A-grades, despite heavy opposition from students The proposal has rocked the Ivy League school, where 60 per cent of undergrad grades were an A in the academic year ending mid-2025 2 -MIN READ 2 -MIN 1 Bloomberg Published: 1:19am, 21 May 2026 More than two-thirds of Harvard University faculty voted to cap the proportion of A-grades in undergraduate courses, instituting one of the strongest policies against grade inflation in US higher education in decades. Starting in the fall of 2027, no more than 20 per cent of a class, plus four additional students, can receive an A. Just under 70 per cent of the votes were in favour of the policy in a week-long electronic vote that concluded on Tuesday. The vote represents a “large mandate for change,” said Alisha Holland, co-chair of the faculty panel that proposed the policy. She said the margin was more lopsided than she expected, adding that the vast majority of eligible faculty cast a vote. Advertisement Holland urged faculty to start revisiting their assignments and grading systems ahead of implementation of the new initiative. “The hope is that the work will begin now, so that when the policy goes into effect faculty and students are prepared for the transition.” The proposal has rocked the Ivy League school, where roughly 60 per cent of undergraduate grades were an A in the academic year that ended in mid-2025. Advertisement Select Voice Select Speed 00:00 00:00 1.00"
